Samuel J. Ling, Jeff Sanny and William Moebs. University Physics Volume 2. [Publisher book and contents](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-introduction) · [Preface and edition notices](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/preface).

Read chapters 5–16 in full, including worked examples, conceptual questions and substantial problems: the complete electricity and magnetism part of this introductory volume. Chapters 1–4 are the thermodynamics part, useful at the later thermal interfaces. Follow named topics when another edition changes numbering.

The current publisher material identifies CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, with component exceptions and additional website conditions. Preserve the actual edition's notices rather than assuming an earlier OpenStax licence applies. Publisher contents and notices were checked on 29 October 2026. This Record links the work; no book, figures or answers are bundled.

**Return:** [Electromagnetic sources — complete books, courses, methods and research](../Sources.lingua#r_6XW0N80NM6WSDWED10N8N7WX3P).

**Third physics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** rechecked the current [preface and rights notice](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/preface) and selected text in [1.1, temperature/equilibrium](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-1-temperature-and-thermal-equilibrium), [1.2, thermometry/scales](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-2-thermometers-and-temperature-scales), [2.1, the molecular ideal-gas model](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas), [2.2, pressure and kinetic moments](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed), [2.3, heat capacity](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-3-heat-capacity-and-equipartition-of-energy), [3.1, systems](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-1-thermodynamic-systems), [3.2, work](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-2-work-heat-and-internal-energy) and [3.3, the first law](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-3-first-law-of-thermodynamics). This is selected inspection, not a complete thermal/electromagnetic book or exercise audit.

Read these introductory thermal selections with explicit model limits. Use Yan 1.2’s fixed-matter definition for closed systems and distinguish isolation; the common core does not adopt the loose conflation in this volume’s 3.1 prose. Thermal equilibrium means no sustained net heat transfer under the relevant conditions, not the cessation of microscopic exchanges. Rigid boundaries eliminate volume work, not every work mechanism. The current SI kelvin definition is checked through the shared metrology source rather than inferred from historical thermometer descriptions. The finite particle tables and path investigation are original, and their matching moments do not establish equilibrium or validate a real gas. All source media remain external.


**Third engineering-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** freshly inspected selected definitions and formula context in the publisher's [section 1.6](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-6-mechanisms-of-heat-transfer), [7.2](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-2-electric-potential-and-potential-difference), [9.1](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-1-electrical-current) and [9.4](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-4-ohms-law). These supply conduction, voltage/current definitions and the empirical scope of the ohmic relation. Current publisher attribution/CC BY-NC-SA and additional-use notices were rechecked; do not infer blanket permissions from free access. The original two-node and transient investigations derive their own hypothetical models and examples. No full-book, apparatus or component-conformance audit is claimed. Keep source media external.


**Fifth physics-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** freshly checked the official preface/attribution and selected text/equations in sections 5.1 (charge/accounting), 5.3–5.4 (Coulomb law and finite-source fields), 7.2–7.3 (potential/work and finite-source sums), 8.1/8.3 (capacitance and work/storage), 9.1/9.4/9.5 (current, empirical Ohmic response and power), and 10.5 (the one-capacitor RC equation/solution). The first attempted 5.1 and RC URLs were incorrect; the publisher contents/search supplied the correct 5-1-electric-charge and 10-5-rc-circuits pages, which were inspected. This is bounded passage inspection, not a complete chapter/figure/example audit. The common lessons qualify capacitance as a linear charge–voltage response coefficient, not a maximum charge rating; they derive storage for constant C, state passive power signs explicitly, and restrict charge-transfer statements to their ordinary matter setting. General field-energy and vector-calculus claims retain prerequisites. The finite-source, two-capacitor, parameter-rival and loading investigations and programs are original; no source experiment or numerical example is claimed reproduced. Full chapter assignments remain. Current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, component exceptions and additional website-use conditions are retained; no source media is bundled.

**Common magnetic/inductive inspection — 1 November 2026:** read selected [11.3](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field) uniform-field motion, [13.3](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-3-motional-emf) opening sliding-bar/emf/energy account and [14.4](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-4-rl-circuits) RL equations and Example 14.4. The last example was independently recalculated with rigorous exponential bounds and a separate Decimal evaluation. Only these bounded passages are claimed inspected, not every figure, experiment or chapter exercise. The [preface](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/preface) rechecks current CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and component/website qualifications. New examples distinguish induced circulating emf from passive voltage drop, fixed from moving loops and transient storage from heating. Earlier identity/assignments remain; no source media or author code is bundled.

